/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/*-------------------------- File succinct.dox -----------------------------*/
/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/** \addtogroup area_succinct */
/** \defgroup theory_succinct Theory of succinct structures
 \ingroup area_succinct

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/succinct` realize the compressed data
 structures of chapter 15, Compressed Data Structures, of Ferragina's book, and
 the representation of Elias and Fano of its chapter 11, Integer Coding, beyond
 the book of Montresor, which does not treat them: the vectors of bits with
 rank and select, the sequences of integers and of symbols searched in their
 compressed form, and the trees and the graphs that take a few bits per node or
 per edge and are navigated without being decoded. This page collects what the
 headers take for granted: the definitions and the notation shared by all of
 them, with the lower bounds against which their space is measured (see
 \ref theory_succinct_notation), the realizations and the costs of their
 operations (see \ref theory_succinct_repr), and, for each family, the property
 on which its correctness rests and the argument behind its bounds (from
 \ref theory_succinct_rank to \ref theory_succinct_learned). The pseudocode of
 each algorithm is instead on the page of its header, in the comment of its
 function, together with the chapter of Ferragina's book, or the paper, that it
 follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_succinct_notation Definitions and notation

 A vector of bits \f$ B[ 0 , m ) \f$ with \f$ n \f$ 1s has, for
 \f$ b \in \{ 0 , 1 \} \f$, the operations
 \f[
   \mathrm{rank}_b( i ) = | \{ j < i : B[ j ] = b \} | , \qquad
   \mathrm{select}_b( k ) = \min\{ i : \mathrm{rank}_b( i + 1 ) = k \} ,
   \tag{1}
 \f]
 i.e., the number of the bits \f$ b \f$ before the place \f$ i \f$ and the
 place of the \f$ k \f$-th bit \f$ b \f$, with \f$ 1 \le k \f$ up to the
 number of the bits \f$ b \f$; hence
 \f$ \mathrm{rank}_b( \mathrm{select}_b( k ) ) = k - 1 \f$, and
 \f$ \mathrm{rank}_0( i ) = i - \mathrm{rank}_1( i ) \f$. The places count
 from 0 in the library, while Ferragina's book counts them from 1, so that
 its \f$ \mathrm{Rank}_1 \f$ and \f$ \mathrm{Select}_1 \f$ are the
 \f$ \mathrm{rank}_1 \f$ and \f$ \mathrm{select}_1 \f$ here with the places
 shifted by one. The
 two operations are the primitives of every other structure of the area:
 a structure that encodes its objects as bits uses \f$ \mathrm{select} \f$
 to go from the number of an object to its place in the bits, and
 \f$ \mathrm{rank} \f$ to go back.

 A representation of the objects of a class of \f$ N \f$ elements needs
 \f$ Z = \lceil \log_2 N \rceil \f$ bits for some of them, the
 <em>information-theoretic lower bound</em>; it is <em>succinct</em> if it
 takes \f$ Z + o( Z ) \f$ bits, <em>compact</em> if it takes \f$ O( Z ) \f$
 bits, and in both cases it is asked to answer its queries in about the time
 of the plain representation. The bounds of the area are the following
 three. The vectors of \f$ m \f$ bits are \f$ 2^m \f$, and those with
 \f$ n \f$ 1s are \f$ \binom{m}{n} \f$, with
 \f[
   \log_2 \binom{m}{n} \le m H_0( B ) , \qquad
   H_0( B ) = \frac{n}{m} \log_2 \frac{m}{n} + \frac{m - n}{m} \log_2
   \frac{m}{m - n} , \tag{2}
 \f]
 since \f$ \binom{m}{n} p^n ( 1 - p )^{m - n} \le 1 \f$ for
 \f$ p = n / m \f$; the sets of \f$ n \f$ integers of \f$ [ 0 , u ) \f$
 are \f$ \binom{u}{n} \f$, i.e., about
 \f$ n \log_2( u / n ) + n \log_2 e \f$ bits when \f$ n \f$ is much smaller
 than \f$ u \f$; the ordered trees of \f$ n \f$ nodes (and the binary trees
 of \f$ n \f$ nodes) are the Catalan number
 \f$ C_n = \binom{2 n}{n} / ( n + 1 ) \approx 4^n / ( n^{3/2} \sqrt{\pi} ) \f$,
 i.e., \f$ 2 n - \Theta( \log n ) \f$ bits.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ B \f$, \f$ m \f$, \f$ n \f$</td>
     <td>a vector of bits, its length and the number of its 1s</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathrm{rank}_b( i ) \f$, \f$ \mathrm{select}_b( k ) \f$</td>
     <td>the bits \f$ b \f$ in \f$ B[ 0 , i ) \f$ and the place of the
         \f$ k \f$-th of them, as in (1); <tt>rank1()</tt>,
         <tt>select1()</tt>, <tt>rank0()</tt>, <tt>select0()</tt> in the
         code</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ H_0 \f$</td>
     <td>the empirical entropy of order 0 of a vector of bits, as in (2),
         or of a sequence of symbols of frequencies \f$ f_c \f$,
         \f$ \sum_c ( f_c / n ) \log_2( n / f_c ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ x_0 , \ldots , x_{n-1} \f$, \f$ u \f$</td>
     <td>a non-decreasing sequence of integers and the bound of its items,
         which lie in \f$ [ 0 , u ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \ell \f$, \f$ L \f$, \f$ H \f$</td>
     <td>the low bits of an item of Elias-Fano, the vector of the low parts
         and that of the high parts in unary</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \sigma \f$, \f$ w \f$</td>
     <td>the size of an alphabet of symbols, and the bits of a symbol of a
         wavelet matrix, \f$ \sigma \le 2^w \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ E( i ) \f$</td>
     <td>the excess of a sequence of parentheses at the place \f$ i \f$,
         the number of <tt>'('</tt> less the number of <tt>')'</tt> in
         \f$ B[ 0 , i ] \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \lvert x \rvert \f$, \f$ \mathrm{popcount}( x ) \f$</td>
     <td>the number of the 1s of a word of 64 bits, its sideways
         sum</td></tr>
 </table>

 The bits are packed in words of 64 bits by `BitVector`, the bit \f$ i \f$
 in the bit \f$ i \bmod 64 \f$ of the word \f$ \lfloor i / 64 \rfloor \f$,
 and the analysis is in the model of the word RAM with words of
 \f$ \Theta( \log m ) \f$ bits, in which the sideways sum of a word and the
 place of its \f$ k \f$-th 1 cost \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time (one instruction for
 the first, a few broadword operations for the second, as
 \ref theory_succinct_rank shows).

 \section theory_succinct_repr Realizations and their costs

 The vectors of bits with rank and select are five, which trade the space
 of the directory against the number of accesses to the memory of a query.

 <table>
 <caption>The vectors of bits with rank and select and their costs, for
          \f$ m \f$ bits</caption>
 <tr><th>Realization</th><th>Space beyond \f$ m \f$</th>
     <th><tt>rank1( i )</tt></th><th><tt>select1( k )</tt></th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>RankSelect</tt>, <tt>LazyRankSelect</tt></td>
     <td>25% for the rank, 9.4% to 18.8% for the select of each
         value</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$, one entry and one word</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ when the bits are spread evenly,
         \f$ O( \log( m / 256 ) ) \f$ in a sparse group</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>CompactRankSelect</tt></td>
     <td>3.125%, and less than 0.8% for the select of each value</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$, one entry and the 8 words of a line</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ when the bits are spread evenly,
         \f$ O( \log( m / 4096 ) ) \f$ in the worst case</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>InterleavedRankSelect</tt></td>
     <td>3.125% inside the lines, and less than 1.2% for the select of
         each value</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$, one line</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ when the bits are spread evenly, the lines between
         two samples in the worst case</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>RRRBitVector</tt></td>
     <td>the whole vector in \f$ m H_0( B ) + 0.175 m \f$ bits</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 32 + b ) \f$, blocks of \f$ b = 63 \f$ bits</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \log( m / 2016 ) + 32 + b ) \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 `RankSelect` is usually preferable when the queries dominate, since its
 rank reads one entry and one word and its select one sample and a few
 words; `CompactRankSelect` and `InterleavedRankSelect` cost about seven
 times fewer bits and a sum of up to 8 words per rank, the second one with
 the counts in the line of the bits, so that a rank misses the cache once
 instead of twice; `RRRBitVector` is the one to choose when the 1s (or the
 0s) are rare, since its space follows (2) instead of \f$ m \f$, at the
 price of the decoding of a block at each query. The lazy form of
 `RankSelect` builds the inventories of the select at the first select of
 each value, for a structure that may only rank.

 <table>
 <caption>The sequences and their costs</caption>
 <tr><th>Realization</th><th>Space in bits</th><th>Access</th>
     <th>Search</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>EliasFano</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ n ( 2 + \lceil \log_2( u / n ) \rceil ) \f$, plus the directory
         of \f$ H \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td><tt>nextGEQ()</tt> in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ expected,
         \f$ O( \log c ) \f$ for a bucket of \f$ c \f$ items</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>PartitionedEliasFano</tt></td>
     <td>at most that of Elias-Fano on each block of 128 items, plus a
         record per block</td>
     <td>a scan of the code of a block</td>
     <td><tt>nextGEQ()</tt> by a search of the records and the scan of a
         block</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>WaveletMatrix</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ n w \f$, plus a directory per level</td>
     <td>\f$ O( w ) \f$</td>
     <td>rank, select, quantile and range counts in \f$ O( w ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>HuffmanWaveletTree</tt></td>
     <td>less than \f$ n ( H_0 + 1 ) \f$, plus the directory and
         \f$ O( \sigma ) \f$ words</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \ell_c ) \f$, \f$ H_0 + 1 \f$ on average</td>
     <td>rank and select of \f$ c \f$ in \f$ O( \ell_c ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>MoveStructure</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ O( r ) \f$ words for a permutation of \f$ r \f$ intervals</td>
     <td>the image of a place and its interval in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>the interval of a place by a binary search</td></tr>
 </table>

 <table>
 <caption>The trees and the graphs and their costs, for \f$ n \f$ nodes and
          \f$ m \f$ edges</caption>
 <tr><th>Realization</th><th>Space in bits</th><th>Operations</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>JacobsonTree</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ 2 n + 1 + o( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>the children and the parent of a binary tree in
