     <td>an edge in \f$ O( h ) \f$, the successors and the predecessors of
         a node</td></tr>
 </table>

 Among the trees, LOUDS is usually preferable for the navigation from the
 root down (e.g., in the tries of an index), since its operations are a
 rank and a select each, while the parentheses answer the size of a subtree,
 the depth and the ancestors, which the order by levels does not keep, at the
 price of the searches of the range min-max tree. Among the graphs, the
 codes of Boldi and Vigna exploit the locality and the similarity of the
 lists of the Web, the \f$ k^2 \f$-tree the clustering of the adjacency
 matrix, and give the predecessors too, and Elias-Fano exploits neither and
 gives the fastest random access.

 \section theory_succinct_rank Rank and select

 <em>Lemma 1 (the directory of Jacobson).</em> With superblocks of
 \f$ Z \f$ bits, each with the 1s before it in \f$ \log_2 m \f$ bits, and
 blocks of \f$ z \f$ bits, each with the 1s before it since its superblock
 in \f$ \log_2 Z \f$ bits, one has
 \f[
   \mathrm{rank}_1( i ) = R_S[ \lfloor i / Z \rfloor ] +
   R_b[ \lfloor i / z \rfloor ] + \mathrm{rank}_1 \text{ of }
   B[ z \lfloor i / z \rfloor , i ) , \tag{3}
 \f]
 and with \f$ Z = \log_2^2 m \f$ and \f$ z = \log_2 m / 2 \f$ the counts take
 \f$ o( m ) \f$ bits, with the rank in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time.

 Indeed, the 1s before \f$ i \f$ are those before its superblock, those of
 its superblock before its block, and those of its block before \f$ i \f$,
 which gives (3). The superblocks take
 \f$ ( m / \log_2^2 m ) \log_2 m = m / \log_2 m \f$ bits and the blocks
 \f$ ( 2 m / \log_2 m ) \log_2 \log_2^2 m = O( m \log \log m / \log m ) \f$,
 both \f$ o( m ) \f$; the last term of (3) is read in a table of the
 \f$ 2^z = \sqrt{m} \f$ blocks of \f$ z \f$ bits and of their
 \f$ z \f$ prefixes, \f$ O( \sqrt{m} \log m \log \log m ) \f$ bits. The
 library replaces the table by the sideways sum of a word, an instruction of
 the processor, which lets \f$ z \f$ be the word itself and \f$ Z \f$ a
 constant: `RankSelect` keeps one word per 256 bits, the 40 bits of the
 count of the block and the three counts of its words in 8 bits each, so
 that (3) is one read of the directory and one of the bits, with 25% of
 space more instead of \f$ o( m ) \f$, a fraction that is constant but
 small at every practical size, since \f$ m / \log_2 m \f$ is still 2.5% of
 \f$ m \f$ for \f$ m = 2^{40} \f$. `CompactRankSelect` keeps one entry of
 128 bits per 4096 bits and sums up to 8 words, 3.125%, and
 `InterleavedRankSelect` writes the count of each line of 512 bits in its
 first 16 bits, so that (3) reads the line alone.

 The select is not reduced to the rank by a binary search, which costs
 \f$ O( \log m ) \f$ steps: Clark cuts the 1s in groups of
 \f$ \log_2^2 m \f$ and writes down the places of all the 1s of a group
 that spans more than \f$ \log_2^4 m \f$ bits, which take
 \f$ O( m / \log m ) \f$ bits in all since such groups are at most
 \f$ m / \log_2^4 m \f$; the other groups are cut again, and the last level
 is solved by a table, which gives \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time in \f$ o( m ) \f$
 bits, as Clark shows. The library follows the
 inventories of Vigna, with groups of constant size: a group of
 \f$ 2^\ell \f$ 1s spans 3072 to 6144 bits on average, its first place is
 written in 64 bits and the places of its 1s of rank multiple of
 \f$ 2^{\ell - 5} \f$ in 16 bits from it, so that the select reads one
 place and scans the words from it to the bit asked for. A group that spans
 \f$ 2^{16} \f$ bits or more has offsets that do not fit in 16 bits, and is
 searched by a binary search of the directory of rank between its ends
 instead; a group that spans less is scanned over less than
 \f$ 2^{16} / 64 = 1024 \f$ words, and over a few words when the 1s are
 spread evenly. The search ends in a word, where the place of the
 \f$ k \f$-th 1 is found by the broadword select of Vigna: the sideways
 sums of the 8 bytes are multiplied by \f$ \sum_{j < 8} 2^{8 j} \f$, which
 leaves in the byte \f$ j \f$ the 1s of the bytes up to \f$ j \f$; the bytes
 whose sum exceeds \f$ k \f$ are marked all at once by an addition of
 \f$ 127 - k \f$ to each, and the first marked byte, found by the trailing
 zeros, holds the bit, which is found inside it by clearing its lower 1s.

 \section theory_succinct_compressed Compressed vectors and Elias-Fano

 <em>Lemma 2 (the combinatorial number system).</em> The vectors of
 \f$ b \f$ bits with \f$ c \f$ 1s at the places
 \f$ p_1 < \cdots < p_c \f$ are numbered from 0 to
 \f$ \binom{b}{c} - 1 \f$ by
 \f[
   o = \sum_{t=1}^{c} \binom{p_t}{t} , \tag{4}
 \f]
 their rank in the colexicographic order, and the place \f$ p \f$, scanned
 from \f$ b - 1 \f$ down, holds the \f$ t \f$-th 1, \f$ t \f$ the 1s not yet
 met, if and only if \f$ \binom{p}{t} \le o \f$, the remainder of
 \f$ o \f$ after the 1s above.

 Indeed, the vectors that precede a given one in the colexicographic order
 are, for each \f$ t \f$, those that agree with it above \f$ p_t \f$ and
 have their \f$ t \f$ lowest 1s below \f$ p_t \f$, \f$ \binom{p_t}{t} \f$
 of them; and since
 \f$ \sum_{t' < t} \binom{p_{t'}}{t'} < \binom{p_t}{t} \f$, the highest term
 of (4) is the largest \f$ \binom{p}{c} \le o \f$, which gives the decoding.
 `RRRBitVector` writes each block of \f$ b = 63 \f$ bits as its class
 \f$ c \f$ and its offset (4) in \f$ \lceil \log_2 \binom{b}{c} \rceil \f$
 bits. The product of the \f$ \binom{b}{c_j} \f$ of the blocks counts some
 of the vectors of \f$ m \f$ bits with \f$ n \f$ 1s, and hence by (2) the
 offsets take at most \f$ m H_0( B ) \f$ bits and one more per block,
 i.e., \f$ m H_0( B ) + m / 63 \f$; the classes take \f$ 6 m / 63 \f$, and
 the counts of the superblocks of 32 blocks at most \f$ 128 / 2016 \f$ of
 the bits, which gives the \f$ m H_0( B ) + 0.175 m \f$ bits of the
 table (see \ref theory_succinct_repr).

 <em>Theorem 3 (Elias and Fano).</em> A non-decreasing sequence of
 \f$ n \f$ integers of \f$ [ 0 , u ) \f$, each split into its
 \f$ \ell = \lceil \log_2( u / n ) \rceil \f$ low bits, written in
 \f$ L \f$, and its bucket \f$ \lfloor x_i / 2^\ell \rfloor \f$, written by
 setting the bit
 \f[
   H[ \lfloor x_i / 2^\ell \rfloor + i ] = 1 , \tag{5}
 \f]
 takes at most \f$ 2 n + n \lceil \log_2( u / n ) \rceil \f$ bits, and
 \f$ x_i = 2^\ell ( \mathrm{select}_1( H , i + 1 ) - i ) + L[ i ] \f$.

 In fact, the places (5) are strictly increasing, since the buckets do not
 decrease and \f$ i \f$ grows, and the \f$ i \f$-th 1 of \f$ H \f$ is
 therefore the one of \f$ x_i \f$, from which its bucket is read back by
 subtracting \f$ i \f$. The 0s of \f$ H \f$ before that 1 are the bucket
 itself, so that \f$ H \f$ has \f$ n \f$ 1s and
 \f$ \lfloor ( u - 1 ) / 2^\ell \rfloor + 1 \le n \f$ 0s, since
 \f$ 2^\ell \ge u / n \f$, and \f$ L \f$ has \f$ n \ell \f$ bits. Against
 the \f$ n \log_2( u / n ) + n \log_2 e \f$ bits of the lower bound (see
 \ref theory_succinct_notation), this is less than 2 bits per item more
 when \f$ n \f$ is much smaller than \f$ u \f$. The first item
 \f$ \ge x \f$ (`nextGEQ()`) lies in the bucket
 \f$ h = \lfloor x / 2^\ell \rfloor \f$ or after it: the items of the bucket
 start after the \f$ h \f$-th 0 of \f$ H \f$, which
 \f$ \mathrm{select}_0( H , h ) \f$ finds, and they share the high bits of
 \f$ x \f$, so that their low bits, which are sorted, decide. A bucket holds
 \f$ n 2^\ell / u \le 2 \f$ items on average when the items are spread
 evenly, and the search is \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ expected, and a binary search past
 8 items keeps it \f$ O( \log c ) \f$ in a cluster of \f$ c \f$ items.

 Elias-Fano depends on \f$ n \f$ and \f$ u \f$ alone, and ignores the
 clusters of a sequence. The partitioned form of Ottaviano and Venturini
 (`PartitionedEliasFano`) codes each block of 128 items relative to the end
 of the block before, in the universe \f$ u_j \f$ of the block, and in the
 cheapest of three ways: no bit at all if the block holds every value of
 its range, a vector of \f$ u_j \f$ bits if it is not longer, Elias-Fano
 otherwise. The cost of a block is never above that of Elias-Fano on its
 own universe, and the sum of these costs, about
 \f$ \sum_j m_j ( 2 + \log_2( u_j / m_j ) ) \f$, is at most the cost of
 Elias-Fano on the whole sequence, since \f$ \sum_j u_j \le u \f$ and the
 logarithm is concave (as in (10) below), plus the records of the blocks;
 it is far smaller on the dense runs of an inverted index.

 \section theory_succinct_trees Trees

 A tree is coded by the shape alone, in about the \f$ 2 n \f$ bits of the
 lower bound (see \ref theory_succinct_notation), and its nodes are
 numbered by the places of their bits, so that the data of a node go in a
 vector indexed by its number.

 <em>Lemma 4 (the binary tree of Jacobson).</em> Complete a binary tree of
 \f$ n \f$ nodes with a leaf in place of each missing child, and write by
 levels a 1 for a node and a 0 for a leaf added, in \f$ B[ 1 , 2 n + 1 ] \f$.
 Then the children of the \f$ x \f$-th node are at the places \f$ 2 x \f$
 and \f$ 2 x + 1 \f$, and the item at the place \f$ p \f$ is the
 \f$ \mathrm{Rank}_1( p ) \f$-th node if \f$ B[ p ] = 1 \f$.

 Indeed, the visit by levels lists the root at the place 1 and then the
 children of the nodes in the order of the nodes, two per node, so that the
 children of the first \f$ x - 1 \f$ nodes take the places
 \f$ 2 , \ldots , 2 x - 1 \f$ and those of the \f$ x \f$-th one the next
 two. With rank and select in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time, a child is
 \f$ \mathrm{Rank}_1( 2 \, \mathrm{Select}_1( x ) ) \f$ and the parent
 \f$ \mathrm{Rank}_1( \lfloor \mathrm{Select}_1( x ) / 2 \rfloor ) \f$; hence
 `JacobsonTree` takes \f$ 2 n + 1 + o( n ) \f$ bits, within
 \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ of the lower bound.

 For a tree of any degree, LOUDS (`LoudsTree`) writes the degree \f$ d \f$
 of each node by levels as \f$ 0^d 1 \f$, after a dummy root of degree 1:
 each node is the 0 that its parent writes for it, so that the \f$ k \f$-th
 0 is the \f$ k \f$-th node by levels, and the children of the
 \f$ k \f$-th node are the 0s after the \f$ ( k + 1 ) \f$-th 1. In the
 notation of Ferragina's book, with the nodes and the places from 1,
 \f[
   \mathrm{parent}( x ) = \mathrm{Select}_0( x ) - x , \qquad
   \mathrm{firstChild}( x ) = \mathrm{Select}_1( x ) + 1 - x , \tag{6}
 \f]
 and the degree is the distance of two consecutive 1s less 1, all in
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time and \f$ 2 n + 1 + o( n ) \f$ bits, since the code has
 \f$ n \f$ 0s and \f$ n + 1 \f$ 1s.
 The order by levels keeps the children of a node consecutive, which gives
 the \f$ i \f$-th child at once, but scatters a subtree.

 The balanced parentheses (`BPTree`) write <tt>'('</tt> when a visit in
 depth enters a node and <tt>')'</tt> when it leaves it, so that the nodes
 of a subtree are consecutive in preorder and a node is the pair of its
 parentheses. With the excess \f$ E \f$ of the notation,
 \f[
   \mathrm{findClose}( i ) = \min\{ j > i : E( j ) = E( i ) - 1 \} , \tag{7}
 \f]
 since the parentheses strictly inside the pair of \f$ i \f$ are balanced
 and keep the excess at least \f$ E( i ) \f$; the size of the subtree is
 half the distance of the pair, the depth is \f$ E( i ) - 1 \f$, and the
 parent is the pair that encloses \f$ i \f$, the last \f$ k < i \f$ with
 \f$ E( k ) = E( i ) - 2 \f$, plus 1. Since the excess moves by
 \f$ \pm 1 \f$, the first place with an excess at most a target has exactly
 that excess, and every operation is a search for the first place, forward
 or backward, whose excess reaches a target. The range min-max tree of
 Navarro and Sadakane (`BalancedParentheses`) keeps, for each block of 512
 bits, for each group of 16 blocks and for each node of a complete binary
 tree over the groups, the total excess of its range and its minimum prefix
 excess; a search scans the block of \f$ i \f$, then climbs to the first
 range on its side whose minimum reaches the target and goes down to its
 block, in \f$ O( \log( n / 8192 ) + 16 + 512 / 8 ) \f$ time, since a range
 whose minimum stays above the target holds no answer and is skipped
 whole.

 \section theory_succinct_sequences Sequences of symbols and permutations

 <em>Lemma 5 (the wavelet matrix).</em> Let the level \f$ \ell \f$ of a
 sequence \f$ S \f$ of \f$ w \f$-bit integers hold the bit
 \f$ w - 1 - \ell \f$ of each item, the items passing to the next level
 stably sorted by that bit, the \f$ Z_\ell \f$ with a 0 first. Then the item
 at the place \f$ i \f$ of a level is at the place
 \f[
   \mathrm{rank}_0( B_\ell , i ) \text{ if its bit is 0}, \qquad
   Z_\ell + \mathrm{rank}_1( B_\ell , i ) \text{ if it is 1} , \tag{8}
 \f]
 of the next one, and the items equal to \f$ c \f$ end consecutive and in
 the order of \f$ S \f$ at the last level.

 Indeed, a stable partition by a bit sends the item to the place given by
 the items with its bit before it, which is (8), and keeps the order among
 the items with the same bit; by induction on the levels the items that
 share the first \f$ \ell \f$ bits stay consecutive, in the order of
 \f$ S \f$, and the place of the range of \f$ c \f$ at the last level
 depends on the bits of \f$ c \f$ alone. Hence `access()`, `rank()` and
 `select()` of `WaveletMatrix` follow a place, or the two ends of a range,
 down the \f$ w \f$ levels by (8) (or up by the select), in
 \f$ O( w ) \f$ time with rank and select in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$; the
 \f$ k \f$-th smallest item of a range goes down to the 0s or the 1s
 according to how many 0s the range holds at each level. The wavelet tree
 of Grossi, Gupta and Vitter splits the alphabet in halves at each node
 instead, with the same costs and a pointer per node; shaped by the code of
 Huffman (`HuffmanWaveletTree`, after Mäkinen and Navarro), the symbol
 \f$ c \f$ of frequency \f$ f_c \f$ goes down \f$ \ell_c \f$ levels, and
 the bits of all the nodes are \f$ \sum_c f_c \ell_c < n ( H_0 + 1 ) \f$ by
 the bound of the code of Huffman.

 The move structure of Nishimoto and Tabei (`MoveStructure`) represents a
 permutation \f$ \pi \f$ of \f$ [ 0 , n ) \f$ that shifts each of
 \f$ r \f$ intervals \f$ [ p_i , p_{i+1} ) \f$ by the same amount, such as
 the LF-mapping of a transform of Burrows and Wheeler on its runs, by the
 starts \f$ p_i \f$, their images \f$ q_i \f$ and the interval \f$ d_i \f$
 of each image:
 \f[
   \pi( x ) = q_i + ( x - p_i ) , \qquad x \in [ p_i , p_{i+1} ) . \tag{9}
 \f]
 The interval of \f$ \pi( x ) \f$ is found from \f$ d_i \f$ forward, past
 the starts that the image of the interval \f$ i \f$ holds; the structure is
 balanced when no image holds 4 starts, which the construction obtains by
 cutting at its third start the interval whose image holds 4, at most
 doubling the intervals (Nishimoto and Tabei). Hence each step of a walk
 costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time, in \f$ O( r ) \f$ words instead of the
 \f$ n \f$ of the permutation written in full.

 \section theory_succinct_graphs Graphs

 A graph of \f$ n \f$ nodes and \f$ m \f$ edges, as sorted adjacency lists,
 is a set of \f$ m \f$ pairs among \f$ n^2 \f$, i.e., \f$ \log_2
 \binom{n^2}{m} \approx m \log_2( n^2 / m ) \f$ bits for a graph with no
 regularity. `EliasFanoGraph` writes the edge \f$ ( u , v ) \f$ as the
 integer \f$ u n + v \f$, so that the lists are one increasing sequence of
 \f$ [ 0 , n^2 ) \f$ coded by Theorem 3, plus a second sequence of the
 prefix sums of the outdegrees that tells where each list starts: the
 \f$ i \f$-th successor is an access, and an edge a `nextGEQ()`. Coding each
 list on its own, in its own universe, takes
 \f[
   \sum_u d_u \log_2 \frac{n}{d_u} \le m \log_2 \frac{n^2}{m} , \tag{10}
 \f]
 by the concavity of the logarithm (the inequality of Jensen on the weights
 \f$ d_u / m \f$), i.e., fewer bits when the degrees are skewed, at the
 price of a directory and an offset per list, the form that
 Ferragina's book describes.

 The graphs of the Web have more structure than (10) sees. With the pages
 sorted by URL, most links stay in the host (locality) and the pages of a
 host link to the same pages (similarity): `BVGraph` codes the list of
 \f$ x \f$ as a copy of the list of one of the previous \f$ W \f$ lists, by
 a sequence of runs of bits, then as intervals of consecutive successors
 and as gaps between the rest, in the codes \f$ \gamma \f$ and
 \f$ \delta \f$, about 3 bits per edge on the Web graphs, as Boldi and
 Vigna measure. A list is decoded after its reference, and the chains
 of references are bounded by \f$ R \f$, so that a list of up to \f$ d \f$
 successors is decoded in \f$ O( R d ) \f$ time. The \f$ k^2 \f$-tree of
 Brisaboa, Ladra and Navarro (`K2Tree`) splits the adjacency matrix, padded
 to the side \f$ k^h \ge n \f$, into \f$ k^2 \f$ submatrices recursively,
 marking with 1 those with an edge, and stores the marks by levels: the
 children of the mark at the place \f$ p \f$ start at
 \f$ k^2 \mathrm{rank}_1( p + 1 ) \f$, as in LOUDS, so that an edge is
 tested on a path of \f$ h \f$ marks, and a row or a column in
 \f$ O( \sqrt{m} ) \f$ time on average for edges spread uniformly
 (\f$ O( n ) \f$ in the worst case). Each edge sets at most \f$ k^2 \f$
 marks per level; below the level where the submatrices outnumber the
 edges these marks are distinct, which gives the bound
 \f$ k^2 m ( \log_{k^2}( n^2 / m ) + O( 1 ) ) \f$ in the worst case (see
 \ref theory_succinct_repr), as Brisaboa, Ladra and Navarro prove, and far
 fewer bits when the edges cluster and the empty submatrices are large.

 \section theory_succinct_learned Sequences compressed by a model of lines

 Elias-Fano spends on each item of a sequence the same bits, whatever the
 regularity of the gaps; the LA-vector of Boffa, Ferragina and Vinciguerra
 (`LAVector`) spends them on the distance of each item from a line instead.
 The points \f$ ( i , x_i ) \f$ of a non-decreasing sequence are covered by
 the fewest segments whose lines are within \f$ \epsilon \f$ of each of
 their points, the optimal piecewise linear approximation of the PGM-index
 (see \ref theory_search_memory), and each item is stored as its
 correction with respect to the line of its segment.

 <em>Lemma 6 (the corrections).</em> Let the segment \f$ j \f$, over the
 places \f$ [ p_j , p_{j+1} ) \f$, have a line
 \f$ f( i ) = x_{p_j} + \gamma + \alpha ( i - p_j ) \f$ with
 \f$ | f( i ) - x_i | \le \epsilon \f$ for each of its places, and let
 \f$ d_i = x_i - x_{p_j} - \lfloor \alpha ( i - p_j ) \rfloor \f$. Then
 the \f$ d_i \f$ take at most \f$ 2 \epsilon + 2 \f$ values, and with
 \f$ \beta_j = x_{p_j} + \min_i d_i \f$
 \f[
   x_i = \beta_j + \lfloor \alpha ( i - p_j ) \rfloor + C[ i ] , \quad
   0 \le C[ i ] < 2^c \text{ for } \epsilon = 2^{c-1} - 1 . \tag{11}
 \f]

 Indeed, \f$ x_i - x_{p_j} - \alpha ( i - p_j ) \f$ lies in
 \f$ [ \gamma - \epsilon , \gamma + \epsilon ] \f$, and taking off the
 integer part of \f$ \alpha ( i - p_j ) \f$ instead of the whole of it
 adds less than 1; hence the \f$ d_i \f$ are integers in an interval of
 length less than \f$ 2 \epsilon + 2 \f$, at most \f$ 2 \epsilon + 2 =
 2^c \f$ of them, and \f$ C[ i ] = d_i - \min_i d_i \f$ takes \f$ c \f$
 bits. The slope \f$ \alpha \f$ is kept in a `double`, whose rounding may
 spread the \f$ d_i \f$ by a few units on items of 64 bits; `LAVector`
 computes the corrections with the rounded slope and gives each segment the
 width \f$ w_j \f$ of the range of its own corrections, so that (11) holds
 exactly, with \f$ w_j \f$ in place of \f$ c \f$ (\f$ c \f$, or one bit
 more). A segment takes 44 bytes (the base, the slope, the place of its
 corrections and their width, its first place and its first item), and the
 two tables that find a segment by a place and by a value take about 4 bytes
 per segment each; hence the sequence takes
 \f$ \sum_j w_j ( p_{j+1} - p_j ) + 416 m \f$ bits for \f$ m \f$ segments.

 The access to \f$ x_i \f$ is (11) once the segment of \f$ i \f$ is known,
 which the table of the segment of every \f$ 2^t \f$-th place gives in
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ expected time for \f$ 2^t \approx n / m \f$. The rank of
 \f$ x \f$ (the place of the first item \f$ \ge x \f$) first finds the
 last segment whose first item is below \f$ x \f$, through a table of the
 first segment of each bucket of the high bits of the items, as the
 buckets of Elias-Fano; then, since by (11)
 \f$ \beta_j + \lfloor \alpha ( i - p_j ) \rfloor \le x_i <
 \beta_j + \lfloor \alpha ( i - p_j ) \rfloor + 2^{w_j} \f$, the answer is
 among the places where the line goes from \f$ x - 2^{w_j} \f$ to
 \f$ x + 1 \f$, about \f$ 2^{w_j} / \alpha \f$ of them, which a binary
 search on the corrections settles in \f$ O( w_j - \log_2 \alpha ) \f$
 steps (the whole segment when the floating point misplaces the window,
 which the search checks on the two items at its ends).

 Against Elias-Fano, which takes \f$ 2 + \lceil \log_2( u / n ) \rceil \f$
 bits per item, the LA-vector wins when the items follow lines closely: on
 the places of a pattern repeated with some noise, on the keys of a process
 that grows at a steady rate, on the dense runs of an inverted index, where
 \f$ m \f$ is small and \f$ c \f$ is the logarithm of the noise rather
 than of the average gap. On gaps drawn at random no line follows the items
 better than the average gap does, the corrections need about
 \f$ \log_2( u / n ) \f$ bits as the low bits of Elias-Fano, and the
 segments are an overhead; `tuned()` tries the widths up to that of
 Elias-Fano and keeps the one of the fewest bits, but the LA-vector of the
 paper goes further and chooses the width segment by segment, by a shortest
 path over the segments of all the widths, which is not realized here.

